---
/**
 * Community (FUTURE_GATES FG-021: the trigger is real — a public channel and
 * issue trackers exist). Links only; no forum is built into the site. Routing
 * follows the SAIPEN HQ branding contract: Discord for conversation, GitHub
 * Issues for reproducible bugs and durable requests.
 */
import MaturityBadge from '../../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import { sourceUrl } from '../../lib/canonical';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Community"
  tag="/community/"
  description="Where to talk about SAIPEN, where to file bugs, and how to propose a protocol change."
>
  <h1>Community</h1>
  <p><MaturityBadge level="stable" /></p>

  <div class="cards">
    <article class="card">
      <header class="card__head"><h2 class="card__title">Discuss</h2></header>
      <div class="card__body">
        <p>
          The SAIPEN Community on Discord: quick questions, screenshots, rough ideas and cross-project feedback.
        </p>
        <p class="card__links">
          <a class="w-button w-button--small" href={SITE.community} rel="noopener" data-external>Join the Discord</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <header class="card__head"><h2 class="card__title">Report</h2></header>
      <div class="card__body">
        <p>
          Reproducible bugs and durable feature requests belong in GitHub Issues, so they do not disappear into chat
          history. Each ecosystem project has its own tracker.
        </p>
        <p class="card__links">
          <a class="w-button w-button--small" href={SITE.issues} rel="noopener" data-external>Open SAIPEN issues</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <header class="card__head"><h2 class="card__title">Contribute</h2></header>
      <div class="card__body">
        <p>
          SAIPEN is a specification first: most contributions change the protocol documents, a phase file or the
          conformance tooling. Read the contributing guide before proposing a change.
        </p>
        <p class="card__links">
          <a class="w-button w-button--small" href={sourceUrl('CONTRIBUTING.md')} rel="noopener" data-external>Read CONTRIBUTING.md</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>Proposing a protocol change</h2>
  <p>Every idea is first run through the litmus test:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Does it make the transition between agents more reliable?</li>
    <li>Does it make the behaviour of different models more uniform?</li>
    <li>Does it reduce the probability of context loss?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Two "no" answers put it out of scope for the protocol, however useful it is elsewhere. A good report names the file
    and section, shows concrete evidence — a quote, a command and its output, a breaking scenario — and says what you
    expected instead. Normative changes need a changelog entry and, where practical, validator or scenario coverage,
    and the canonical validator (<code>python tools/validate.py</code>) must pass.
  </p>

  <h2>Already decided</h2>
  <p>These were proposed and evaluated before; reopening them needs new evidence, not a new request:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>turning SAIPEN into a distributed consensus system;</li>
    <li>a machine-parseable LOG marker grammar beyond the existing line skeleton;</li>
    <li>a <code>saipen doctor</code> command that would duplicate <code>validate</code> and <code>status</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conduct</h2>
  <p>
    The project's <a href={sourceUrl('CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md')} rel="noopener" data-external>code of conduct</a> applies in
    the repository and in the community channels.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
